Consequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120279/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120279"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "a4e5b9db-0b7c-56ca-af3e-896e2c8ee0b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "28d0f419-57de-547f-8d8e-54d210b142fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7be7c3-ef06-563e-90a1-fdd90054876a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326013/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-326013/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-326013/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "3ca5f72c-bdbb-5dac-8df8-acc9530dfad7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fae648c-fad5-586a-9449-3fa74de70bd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326013/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-326013/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326013" :question/id #uuid "7d681eea-b9ef-5eca-b425-7d37983545ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-326013" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-326013/field-1" "q-326013/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-326013"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-327075"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-322576"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568/field-1/4582dbd6025ba0c110f8e23964efc62e8612751348ffad003e8834a5780c1878" :answer/id #uuid "9baedfb5-b338-52e2-af97-1516e5fe58c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568/field-1/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a" :answer/id #uuid "9ce7c869-1909-5b29-a677-65749f4689cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc943c4e-d454-5683-a36f-5682f9921a7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-322568/field-1/4582dbd6025ba0c110f8e23964efc62e8612751348ffad003e8834a5780c1878" :answer-field/choices ["q-322568/field-1/4582dbd6025ba0c110f8e23964efc62e8612751348ffad003e8834a5780c1878" "q-322568/field-1/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568/field-2/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a" :answer/id #uuid "c73b0068-3c9b-58bf-bb3d-96edbc18714f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "623c8595-5f6a-5c71-834e-fd37420c548e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-322568/field-2/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a" :answer-field/choices ["q-322568/field-2/fba3f071a3bf374a14af37c3a2bf03fdd1045070379d4ec39535030fd010d58a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-322568" :question/id #uuid "8871e56f-b40f-5383-aecc-edf37892d8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-322568" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/7e92a09f8448f4d320f86656bc0774e6167b96156041c32eb7a64ba5e8820128.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=3:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{3}{7}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}d+\\frac{3}{7}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-322568/field-1" "q-322568/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-322568"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-322582/field-1/dd04b161c56bcfc6dd7f009cdbb4a76449d8c8e0b62bf12c7a4d4d93bf7b9e5e" :answer/id #uuid "9b001ae9-81ad-59c8-8c5b-8cbfd28e8a99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-322582/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "30c6e253-2c1f-5e7e-9af3-50c7c7d9502d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-322582/field-1/dd04b161c56bcfc6dd7f009cdbb4a76449d8c8e0b62bf12c7a4d4d93bf7b9e5e" :answer-field/choices ["q-322582/field-1/dd04b161c56bcfc6dd7f009cdbb4a76449d8c8e0b62bf12c7a4d4d93bf7b9e5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-322582/field-2/287932369c00835e3ffc94b5f77d978b48de2ebcb85616f58faea5e94528590f" :answer/id #uuid "bf63cd95-a5d1-544a-bb7a-3e6f68873ded" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-322582/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e4272d8-a7bf-5d07-8430-6379ce19a61b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-322582/field-2/287932369c00835e3ffc94b5f77d978b48de2ebcb85616f58faea5e94528590f" :answer-field/choices ["q-322582/field-2/287932369c00835e3ffc94b5f77d978b48de2ebcb85616f58faea5e94528590f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-322582" :question/id #uuid "33f4ca89-dab6-5a7c-b774-67f820e3804d" :question/math-academy-id "q-322582" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/76ddb750ca447fd7f73f2481ecd46bb53eba12e392e81e8b64c2c23589e11694.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=z,$ and $Y$ divides $\\overset{―}{KZ}$ in the ratio $KY:YZ=10:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OY}$ via the vectors $k$ and $z.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OY}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$k+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{KY}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OKZ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{KZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{KO}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-k+z \\\\  & =z-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $KY:YZ=10:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{KY}=\\frac{10}{17}\\overset{\\to }{KZ}=\\frac{10}{17}(z-k).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY} \\\\  & =k+\\frac{10}{17}(z-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{17}k+\\frac{10}{17}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-322582/field-1" "q-322582/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-322582"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection/4a2898cb4dc390c48acdc534dcc02514def4778f221e33c93d0f912af98c7ea6" :answer/id #uuid "d9bc1f90-65f7-5311-bdee-98bcc5bcbdb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{4}h+\\frac{1}{4}i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection/a6f61ecd0a40c332501fb319a1afc660f928747653858a184780b31ed7b056df" :answer/id #uuid "be17862a-48e0-5b2f-a69b-dd7014b0f082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{8}h+\\frac{3}{4}i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection/1cb45edf9ceb86c0b143028e011a026bf8baf4dca96143f591c25341bfa69bb5" :answer/id #uuid "ed5bc7a0-60e9-501d-84bb-229c657a63b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}h+\\frac{1}{2}i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection/fd8c8f4c5d75db5706cb24d15a2044dfaa3a96a186dd0e878c9efb551786a5a3" :answer/id #uuid "9575391e-2027-5cdf-a37a-7d393201587e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection/8fc3afe413bca23e78e2fdb17b7498b3c654421ba2c00f893617e966d225385b" :answer/id #uuid "7c796899-7c91-5113-96b1-20fca7828264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{5}{8}h+\\frac{3}{8}i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3164c9bf-3cc8-5754-9865-06be6104b342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-120304/selection/fd8c8f4c5d75db5706cb24d15a2044dfaa3a96a186dd0e878c9efb551786a5a3" :answer-field/choices ["q-120304/selection/4a2898cb4dc390c48acdc534dcc02514def4778f221e33c93d0f912af98c7ea6" "q-120304/selection/a6f61ecd0a40c332501fb319a1afc660f928747653858a184780b31ed7b056df" "q-120304/selection/1cb45edf9ceb86c0b143028e011a026bf8baf4dca96143f591c25341bfa69bb5" "q-120304/selection/fd8c8f4c5d75db5706cb24d15a2044dfaa3a96a186dd0e878c9efb551786a5a3" "q-120304/selection/8fc3afe413bca23e78e2fdb17b7498b3c654421ba2c00f893617e966d225385b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-120304" :question/id #uuid "c0b691a2-9da7-5f9d-b601-8f5daf61f352" :question/math-academy-id "q-120304" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6560a7f05ae95638cbfe902dfa022b2d8de6b07c6e1e6f85051ccf87b92ec338.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=5:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=5:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{5}{8}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{5}{8}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120304/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120304"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-42669"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5212"] :question/problem "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when two known vectors are not parallel, a linear combination of them is unique. So, once we rewrite ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5c644b15efcf5a4274de23242a722604ead2ff800ea29ba7a650729227fefdf0.png) in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a7b9033c421072576aaf959980d778c319a8ed0f849e08db644b63bc6994088c.png) we can match coefficients to determine the unknown scalar.\n\nTo find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/166c90a9c29f99347bff6b397f31d705ec6407a19b4d71246f98308bbd7a5473.png) we want to match the expression\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/fc79367916dd7671c17de4f40b3d83f91d41c2f9988645964de0b53e5b11ab9b.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/91f686c6ca02e85c386cb0f9e80b66beface446d3a541952e0ac91738ad0db37.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d54b9a8919c7b6106fcf5770c62b1823605d7be98a55d5333e73bcfb7528997a.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/6e1bbbe9eb66856824ff70d16cc2c077519d361546b226a67146e20f15a24dd0.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ea517ac4ca8e0bcf7ba48f23e66f100323d5485f1221219aacfbea9148f4a1af.png)\n\nNote that in ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f1c944e6afa4e9cd84bc68004a2bfd918d81498a727aeb7c56ec627806bf93f6.png), we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b7ac390c4e43b2fa2fbb4063632f98869256cea877caa5e5dad4aad33ae94722.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/7e5f2b01647ab01052fff18ab760273eebe19d47b56051b1953adf587d7f9fb3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/784225d11bf94b7fbd67f8e2395695141eb75b1cb44f89d49b29bb3a2031534b.png) have the same direction\n\nHence, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/21261c8ad0d86a9e7300ffc58c57fd76beef9f627492bcfe2051d5e3cfd82a4e.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ae9c98ded3aa4317a729571710ad38d973a2d67b5faeb9ad26af7c24ee5c3193.png)\n\nConsequently, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/aee3287ad64a654880216142756811bd4d5232b3dba2f48378b8b1c5241e4316.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/466863f8c7522ac89896a4233a823be02940535097c28328fa2c9e9e1f4bc3d8.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/4ac145b710c5354b44a4ea1dc820d70f54b5c17985f078fce6313b671ec26fd9.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-26144"] :question/problem "Consider the picture below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d4f18c3567d0c55d3d6e002fbe2c6ac50b97b7adc14b016c8e95006de98ca15d.png)\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/9b8abad76e493e2619e6b3659e09549981e0d53c725dc0303b6c715d180bf79d.png) find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/32816d59754430b4ea5a50216f2534c38256778a141216a77004eaa2a3900da0.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ae4e47577b23025037443e79415dc50ff81b984cac5c4bb82421f985cfe4d69b.png) we want to match the expression\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ae08dae224f819a1bba0cf5a55e02b83332057a97eeee712c00aec464f5fb2f0.png)\n\nWe are given that one of the components is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/54cbe8d80ab4891ad72ed68be3b002fcbb2002bfb6929882023b3eb6a00d694b.png) So, we first draw the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/8696f37fa8438a1b533eba0b398deb2c9c359182f0f88ae283510955c091a8da.png) from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/412aa5679522fa7148c8d9557fe8e45b36467b8b7052a06a4f792f2afda8908b.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/65a6b5743ab9b6ef820744d0d6601b7dfd8de08a3f895661d96d2fe2cab8e0a7.png)\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) be the endpoint of the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d8c5a62b30eeb1bc2e35d95dfc5d964dd00e512f3f7b1b53379755b1ebff5129.png) Then\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/bf806ea325f77a377f26e5592a57533e4c67f3374cb5f8a947cc40c017f2553c.png)\n\nIn ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/934e201a8788fc6c8ff3a81d3946b86454ce5a69d0d9d1466cf0fff9a0de6ef5.png) we have:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/e3d6c666e1d968e975687d210ce4d314f2ac34ba1352c493edbfd7171591196b.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/554f5985d5ce0ea6e9de573dc63244a4d3d9c757996985514aaf1aa0086f7b46.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/d9dc9bcbe34586fe4e368536ed74377c765274e2e5b008e1fee5d65e3b5008c0.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f741fd94bd2796a620e8f36b686a794d9b897669ef096fda632293f1c852ccda.png)\n\nSimilarly,\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/cb4b9bd2f50fd4b2da78369b90c522bc2b7ee20cbafa9460b643889733487c5b.png)\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5fdd95564252a9a4218055ca7670a535233aa026955b7e410700e26c4f7d170c.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1f42d4df263db3845ca2361b8f89c4075e0cdb9b39e5564c39ae82115932a201.png) have opposite directions\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/874fc16afdc829b59ebb3f871e48722f6a11e475577e3195549d68cb2c836d30.png)\n\nAdditionally,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5765a85c90552570f9a2a041f2f324a9fa0ce183d42708344531a13fbee802c8.png)\n\nConsequently,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/ee7def88d1be490478913a454811c3e0dd4ea0f3c93d2bff532d1d112d270e51.png)\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f37c2a4dfc6f3d9d7a3b80b6001b45bb42f00969109be97a94c018d4e69b89a0.png) the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/3720c5887713d3f7a29be384fe5e883390935b2b9b469af626a8dba968f4da8b.png) we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/5c4c54de5cbe2051d295c0e7dd0128d4eb7fedc8a82b7aa3b93d871238bb60d7.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4287"] :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4288"] :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
